{"word": "renaissance", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "renaissance", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ɹəˈneɪs(ə)ns/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ɹəˈneɪsɑːns/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ɹəˈneɪsɒ̃(n)s/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˌɹɛnəˈsɒ̃(n)s/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈɹɛnəˌsɑns/", "tags": ["Canada", "General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˌɹɛnəˈsɑns/", "tags": ["Canada", "General American"]}], "forms": [{"word": "renaissances", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "A rebirth or revival.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "On the other hand, the nationalitarian phenomenon is one in which the struggle against the imperialist powers of occupation has as its object, beyond the clearing of the national territory, the independence and sovereignty of the national State, uprooting in depth the positions of the ex-colonial power— the reconquest of the power of decision in all domains of national life, the prelude to that reconquest of identity which is at the heart of the renaissance undertaken on the basis of fundamental national demands, and ceaselessly contested, by every means available, on every level, and notably on the internal level'.", "reference": "1981, Anouar Abdel-Malek, Social Dialectics: Nation and Revolution, page 13:"}, {"text": "The news of her death is shocking, in part, because she was in the midst of a late-career renaissance. It seemed possible that we would see her continue to play some of these characters, not to mention a raft of new ones, for another generation.", "reference": "2026 January 30, Judy Berman, “Catherine O’Hara Portrayed the Vain and Delusional With a Radical Lack of Vanity”, in TIME, archived from the original on 01 Feb 2026:"}], "synonyms":